<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sant Martí del Canigó</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1009</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Saint-Martin-du-Canigou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sant Martí del Canigó is a Benedictine monastery high on the slopes of the Canigó massif in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, now in the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Pyrénées-Orientales. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wifred-ii-of-cerdanya">Wifred II of Cerdanya</a> founded it, its church was consecrated in 1009, and he later retired there as a monk. Its early Romanesque church and cloister were restored in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sant-marti-del-canigo.jpg" alt="Sant Martí del Canigó" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LeZibou · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Martin_du_Canigou_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
